gm-method: when hunting for 'frozen/cached derived-state assumed identical to a fresh recompute' bugs (CULL_REARM_STRIDE class), the productive search targets are: (1) every place a per-frame expensive derived computation (culling, LOD assignment, shadow map) is gated behind a boolean/counter throttle (autoUpdate=false, frame-stride modulo, distance-hysteresis flag) -- for EACH one, ask 'can the INPUT the derived state depends on change while the gate holds it frozen, via a DIFFERENT code path than the one the gate was designed against'; (2) cross-reference the freeze gate's own inputs (e.g. camera stillness) against sibling systems that keep running on their own cadence during the same window (e.g. a streaming/chunk-loader that adds new render instances on an idle-throttled cadence) -- the freeze was reasoned about only w.r.t. its own gate's trigger (camera movement), not w.r.t. a second independent mutation source (new instances) hitting the SAME frozen object underneath it; (3) verify library semantics directly (don't assume) -- read the vendored dependency's actual gate condition (e.g. `if (this.autoUpdate && !alreadyPerformed) performCulling()`) to confirm the frozen path truly skips the derived recompute for newly-added state, not just for existing state. This is a distinct failure shape from the anomaly-fraction/proxy-metric class and from the stale-test-assertion class: it requires reading the INTERACTION between two independently-reasoned mechanisms (a freeze gate + a streaming loader) that individually look correct.